###### Abstract

We present an analysis of archival Gemini g^{\\prime},\\ r^{\\prime},K and Keck H,\\ K_{s} imaging of this nearby short period binary (P_{B}=95.8 min) 2.87 ms pulsar. The heated companion is clearly detected. Direct pulsar heating provides an acceptable model at the revised >700 pc parallax distance. The relatively shallow light curve modulation prefers an inclination i<40^{\\circ}; this high latitude view provides a likely explanation for the lack of radio signatures of wind dispersion or eclipse. It also explains the low minimum companion mass and, possibly, the faintness of the source in X- and \\gamma-rays.

